\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 \{\uc0\u8232 "question_id": "Q03",\u8232 "marks": [\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 1,\u8232 "mark": "Misleading",\u8232 "evidence": "The proceedings could not be quashed only because the respondents had filed a civil suit with respect to the aforesaid documents.",\u8232 "reason": "It involves forgery and cheating in a civil dispute but rejects the proposition that civil proceedings alone prevent criminal prosecution.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 2,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "several disputes of a civil nature may also contain the ingredients of criminal offences and if so, will have to be tried as criminal offences",\u8232 "reason": "It directly explains that a civil dispute can also support criminal proceedings when criminal ingredients are present.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 3,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "Merely because a civil claim is maintainable does not mean that the criminal complaint cannot be maintained.",\u8232 "reason": "It directly states that the availability of a civil claim does not by itself bar criminal prosecution.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 4,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "Merely because a civil claim is maintainable does not mean that the criminal complaint cannot be maintained.",\u8232 "reason": "It directly addresses whether criminal proceedings can continue despite a civil dispute or remedy.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 5,\u8232 "mark": "Partly",\u8232 "evidence": "any effort to settle civil disputes and claims, which do not involve any criminal offence, by applying pressure through criminal prosecution should be deprecated and discouraged.",\u8232 "reason": "It supports preventing criminal prosecution used to pressure parties over purely civil claims, but does not fully resolve when cheating or forgery allegations remain criminal.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 6,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "the High Court should not hesitate to quash criminal proceedings to prevent abuse of process of court.",\u8232 "reason": "It directly gives the test for quashing where an essentially civil dispute has been given a criminal cloak and a civil remedy exists.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 7,\u8232 "mark": "Partly",\u8232 "evidence": "The Investigating Officer, however, upon investigation of the matter came to the conclusion that the dispute between the parties was a civil dispute.",\u8232 "reason": "It concerns a land dispute involving alleged forgery and cheating but only reports the investigator's conclusion without stating the governing legal rule.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 8,\u8232 "mark": "Partly",\u8232 "evidence": "in the event the court finds that the dispute between the parties is civil in nature, it may not allow the criminal proceedings to go on.",\u8232 "reason": "It recognizes possible termination of criminal proceedings in a civil dispute but expressly declines to formulate a general rule.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 9,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "where the act alleged would predominantly be a civil wrong, such an act does not constitute a criminal offence.",\u8232 "reason": "It directly states that predominantly civil conduct may not constitute a criminal offence, addressing the core issue.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 10,\u8232 "mark": "No",\u8232 "evidence": "Mr. Manish Pitale, learned Advocate appearing for the appellant submitted that",\u8232 "reason": "This passage records an advocate's argument rather than the court's holding on whether criminal law can apply.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 11,\u8232 "mark": "Partly",\u8232 "evidence": "execution of such document (purporting to convey some property of which he is not the owner) is not execution of a false document as defined under section 464 of the Code.",\u8232 "reason": "It addresses forgery arising from a land sale dispute but focuses on the technical definition of a false document rather than the broader civil-criminal question.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 12,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "where the criminal complaints are filed in respect of property disputes of civil in nature only to harass the accused, and to pressurize him in the civil litigation pending",\u8232 "reason": "It directly states that criminal proceedings may be quashed when used abusively to pressure an accused in a civil property dispute.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 13,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "where the criminal complaints are filed in respect of property disputes of civil in nature only to harass the accused, and to pressurize him in the civil litigation pending",\u8232 "reason": "It directly recognizes quashing where criminal proceedings are merely abusive pressure in pending civil property litigation.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 14,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "The purely civil dispute, is sought to be given a colour of a criminal offence to wreak vengeance against the Appellant.",\u8232 "reason": "The court directly characterizes an allegedly purely civil dispute presented as a criminal offence as an abuse of process.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 15,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "The real test is whether the allegations in the complaint disclose the criminal offence of cheating or not.",\u8232 "reason": "It gives the direct test for deciding whether cheating allegations in a civil dispute justify criminal proceedings.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 16,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "A matter which essentially involves dispute of a civil nature should not be allowed to be the subject matter of a criminal offence",\u8232 "reason": "It directly states that an essentially civil dispute should not be converted into a criminal matter.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \}\u8232 ],\u8232 "good_answer_present": "Yes",\u8232 "best_passage_no": 15,\u8232 "caveats": ""\u8232 \}\
